# =============================================================================
# RED RXXM — environment configuration
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values:  cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored and must NEVER be committed. Only this example file is.
#
# Three different places hold configuration for this app. Putting a value in the
# wrong one is the most common setup mistake, so they're separated below:
#
#   SECTION 1  Browser variables      -> this file / .env  (and your host's dashboard)
#   SECTION 2  Server-side secrets    -> Supabase Edge Function secrets, NOT this file
#   SECTION 3  Provider settings      -> Supabase dashboard UI, no variable at all
#   SECTION 4  Not needed yet         -> features that aren't built
#
# Anything with VITE_ in front of it is BUNDLED INTO THE BROWSER and is readable by
# anyone using the app. Never put a secret, service key, or password in a VITE_ value.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 1 — Browser variables (REQUIRED — the app will not start without these)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All three are validated at startup by src/lib/env.ts. If any is missing or empty
# the app now shows an error page naming the problem, rather than a blank screen.

# Your Supabase project URL.
# Where: Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL
# NOTE: this must be the project your real users are in. Check it carefully —
# this repo previously pointed at a different project to the live one.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# The PUBLISHABLE (anon) key — safe for the browser by design. Protected by the
# database's row-level security rules, so those rules must be correct.
# Where: Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API Keys -> publishable / anon
# Do NOT use the secret / service_role key here. That key bypasses all security.
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Comma-separated list of origins allowed to complete a sign-in redirect.
# Anything not listed here is rejected before the request is sent.
# The dev server runs over http, so the local entry must be http, not https.
# Include every origin the app is served from — local, preview, and production.
VITE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8080,https://redroom.space


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 1b — Browser variables (OPTIONAL — each switches a feature on)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are built and wired. Leave any of them blank and the app runs exactly as
# it does now, with that feature simply absent — nothing breaks. Fill one in and
# the feature turns on at the next reload. No code changes needed either way.

# --- Place search & readable location names ---
# Without this, area search can only suggest places already present in your own
# data, and a user's location is stored as raw coordinates like
# "GPS -26.2041, 28.0473". With it, people can search for any suburb or city, and
# locations become real place names.
# Where: account.mapbox.com -> Tokens -> create a token (the default public one works)
# IMPORTANT: restrict the token to your domain under "URL restrictions" before
# using it. This value ships to the browser, so an unrestricted token can be
# used by anyone and billed to you.
VITE_MAPBOX_TOKEN=

# Optional. Limits place search to one country, e.g. "za" for South Africa only.
# Leave blank to search worldwide.
VITE_GEOCODING_COUNTRY=za

# --- Push notifications ---
# Lets you reach people when the app is closed. Free — no account, no provider.
# Generate the key pair yourself (the private half must never be committed):
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# Put the PUBLIC key here. Put the PRIVATE key in Supabase Edge Function secrets
# as VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY, together with VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY and VAPID_SUBJECT
# (e.g. mailto:support@your-domain.com).
#
# Two more steps before this works, both requiring database access:
#   1. Apply supabase/migrations/20260701_001_push_subscriptions.sql
#   2. supabase functions deploy send-push
# Until then, leave this blank and the option stays hidden in Settings.
VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# --- Crash reporting ---
# Without this, a crash is only visible in the affected user's own browser
# console — which is exactly why this app served a blank page to everyone and
# nobody found out. With it, you get an alert with a stack trace.
# Where: sentry.io -> create project -> platform "React" -> copy the DSN
# Safe to expose; a DSN only permits sending reports, not reading them.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 2 — Server-side secrets (do NOT put these in .env)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These belong to the Supabase Edge Functions, which run on Supabase's servers.
# Set them at: Supabase dashboard -> Edge Functions -> Secrets
# They are listed here only so you know the full set that must exist.
#
# Putting any of these in .env would ship them to the browser. They are secrets.

# --- Supplied automatically by Supabase. You do not create these. ---
#   SUPABASE_URL
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#
# --- PayFast (South African payments) — you must create these ---
# Where: payfast.co.za -> log in -> Settings -> Integration
#   PAYFAST_MERCHANT_ID          Your merchant ID
#   PAYFAST_MERCHANT_KEY         Your merchant key
#   PAYFAST_PASSPHRASE           Set one under Settings -> Security. Required —
#                                payments are rejected without a matching passphrase.
#   PAYFAST_SANDBOX              "true" for testing, "false" for real money.
#                                Both the checkout and the payment-confirmation
#                                function read this, so it must be set once and
#                                apply to both.
#
# Also set in the PayFast dashboard (not a variable) — the ITN / notify URL:
#   https://your-project-ref.supabase.co/functions/v1/payfast-itn
# This is how a payment gets confirmed. Without it, money is taken and nothing
# is ever unlocked or credited to anyone.
#
# Before going live, ask PayFast directly whether your content category is
# permitted. Finding out afterwards means a frozen account holding customer money.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 3 — Configured in the Supabase dashboard (no variable anywhere)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These have no entry in any file. They are set through the Supabase UI, and the
# app simply stops working correctly if they're missing.

# --- Google sign-in ---
# 1. console.cloud.google.com -> APIs & Services -> OAuth consent screen
#    (External; scopes: email, profile, openid; then publish it)
# 2. -> Credentials -> Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application
#    Authorised redirect URI must be SUPABASE'S callback, not your own domain:
#      https://your-project-ref.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#    Authorised JavaScript origins: your production URL and http://localhost:8080
#    (Pointing this at your own domain is the usual cause of redirect_uri_mismatch.)
# 3. Supabase -> Authentication -> Providers -> Google -> enable, paste the
#    Client ID and Client Secret.
#
# --- Allowed redirect URLs ---
# Supabase -> Authentication -> URL Configuration
#   Site URL:       https://redroom.space
#   Redirect URLs:  https://redroom.space/auth/callback
#                   http://localhost:8080/auth/callback
# Miss this and sign-in succeeds but lands on an error page.
#
# --- Email delivery (sign-up confirmations, password resets) ---
# Supabase's built-in email is rate-limited and not intended for production;
# without your own sender, confirmation emails throttle or land in spam.
# Get a sender at resend.com (or Postmark / SendGrid), verify your domain by
# adding the DNS records they give you, then:
# Supabase -> Project Settings -> Authentication -> SMTP
#   Host: smtp.resend.com   Port: 465   Username: resend   Password: <api key>
#
# --- Storage buckets ---
# Supabase -> Storage. Access rules for `posts` and `profile-avatars` already
# exist in the migrations, but the buckets themselves were never created, so
# media upload cannot work until they exist.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 3b — Local tooling only (never used by the app itself)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only by scripts run on your own machine — migrations, diagnostics, deploys.
# The app never reads this and it is never sent to the browser: Vite only bundles
# variables whose name starts with VITE_, which is exactly why this one must NOT
# be renamed to VITE_SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN. That would publish it to every visitor.

# Supabase personal access token, for applying migrations and running diagnostics.
# Where: supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens -> Generate new token
#        (shown once, starts with sbp_)
#
# ⚠ This grants control over your ENTIRE Supabase account — every project in every
#   organisation, not just this one. Treat it like a password:
#     • Never commit it. `.env` is git-ignored; keep it that way.
#     • Never paste it into a chat, issue, or screenshot.
#     • Revoke it when the task is done (same page, delete the token).
#   It only needs to exist while migrations are being applied.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 4 — Not needed yet (features that aren't built)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Listed so you can see the full picture and plan costs. Do NOT sign up for these
# yet — nothing reads them, and the streaming choice in particular shapes how the
# feature gets built, so picking before deciding is expensive to undo.

# --- Live video streaming (NOT BUILT — no streaming exists in the app today) ---
# Decide first: broadcast (one to many, a few seconds' delay, cheaper) or
# interactive (guests on stream, sub-second, pricier). Then pick one provider:
#   LiveKit     cloud.livekit.io          -> API key, API secret, websocket URL
#   Cloudflare  dash.cloudflare.com       -> Stream: account ID + API token
#   Mux         dashboard.mux.com         -> token ID + token secret
# The public playback URL would be a VITE_ value; the signing secret would be an
# Edge Function secret, never a VITE_ one.
# VITE_LIVEKIT_URL=
# VITE_STREAM_PLAYBACK_URL=

# --- Content moderation (NOT BUILT — legal exposure, not just a feature) ---
# The app accepts user photos and video and has a sensitive-content flag.
#   Cloudflare CSAM Scanning Tool — free, dashboard -> Trust & Safety.
#     Requires your domain to be on Cloudflare. Treat as mandatory for a public
#     platform accepting user uploads.
#   Hive or AWS Rekognition — for nudity / violence classification. Server-side
#     keys only; these must never be VITE_ values.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not used by the application
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID appears in some setups but nothing in src/ reads it.
# The project reference is already part of VITE_SUPABASE_URL. The Supabase CLI
# reads its own project id from supabase/config.toml instead — and if that file
# disagrees with VITE_SUPABASE_URL, CLI commands will target a different project
# to the one the app talks to. Keep them in sync.
